QEMU Cocoa: flush the view context after surface texture work

What: with_gl_view_ctx() calls glFlush() before it leaves QEMU's view context
(CGL and EGL). Surface switches (cocoa_gl_switch) and surface updates
(cocoa_gl_update) run there, also for every display head.
Why: nothing else flushes that context, and Apple's GL keeps the memory of large
textures made, deleted or uploaded on a context until it is flushed. With virgl
the layer shows the guest's scanout texture and never touches the surface
texture, so every guest mode change (set_scanout -> qemu_console_resize ->
cocoa_gl_switch) left a whole screen texture behind in "IOAccelerator
(graphics)". A guest switching between two large modes in a loop grew QEMU by
about 1.1 GB per switch and never gave it back (20 GB after 18 switches; also
in 2.6.0 and 2.7.0): any guest could use up the Mac's memory. Measured on the
Mac mini's GPU (M4) with the same GL calls (Tests/display/view-texture-churn.c):
3840x2160 <-> 2560x1440 +16 MB per switch, 8000x6000 <-> 7000x5000 +166 MB per
switch; 2560x1600 and smaller did not grow. With the flush all stay flat.
Upstream: the Cocoa GL code comes from the texture-borrowing patch, not upstream
QEMU; not sent.
Tests: Tests/display/test-gl-view-flush.c (build time, Apple's software
renderer: runs with_gl_view_ctx() from the patched cocoa.m and counts texture
work left unflushed; without this patch all 128 checks fail);
Tests/display/view-texture-churn.c (manual, GPU memory per switch, capped).
--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -2629,6 +2629,7 @@
         eglMakeCurrent(qemu_egl_display, egl_surface,
                        egl_surface, gl_view_ctx);
         block();
+        glFlush();
         eglMakeCurrent(qemu_egl_display, EGL_NO_SURFACE,
                        EGL_NO_SURFACE, EGL_NO_CONTEXT);
         return;
@@ -2637,6 +2638,14 @@
 
     CGLSetCurrentContext((CGLContextObj)gl_view_ctx);
     block();
+    /*
+     * OmacVM: hand the work to the driver before leaving the context. Nothing
+     * else flushes this context, and Apple's GL keeps the memory of large
+     * textures (a 4K screen or more) made, deleted or uploaded here until it
+     * is flushed: every guest mode change left a whole screen texture behind,
+     * and a guest switching modes in a loop used up the Mac's memory.
+     */
+    glFlush();
     CGLSetCurrentContext(NULL);
 }
 
